WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.940
In this talk, we want
to go high level.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:16.070
And we want to describe how we
envision the future of Shiny

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:20.960
and what we do to
fuel that future.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:21.940
My name is Filip.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.260
I'm the CEO of Appsilon.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:29.420
And together with me,
we have Marek, our CTO.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:35.930
So we want to share what Appsilon
built to empower everyone

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.960
to create a
spectacular Shiny apps.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.930
All right, so if
you're still with us,

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:51.850
I think we can guess that you
love Shiny just as we do,

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.660
and I personally
believe that Shiny is

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.850
a unique piece of technology.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.140
It empowers thousands of
scientists and data scientists

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.340
worldwide to share, to
first to create, and then

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:14.200
to share their analysis
and useful tools easily.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.340
Through the years, we've
seen some unique examples,

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:24.250
some crazy ones starting
from native iPad applications,

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:28.820
through software as
a service Shiny apps,

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:33.640
and ending up with offline
Shiny applications

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.460
on and on and on.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.880
Just through the
pandemic, we created

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.920
free, publicly available
dashboards, which

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.960
were mentioned in the press.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.060
And on a given day they
received significant traffic.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.910
So I think that
it is clear by now

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:59.530
that Shiny is ready to be
used in different environments

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:03.490
enterprise environment,
internal environment,

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:10.300
external environment, and is
being used by governments, NGOs

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.890
and businesses.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:20.870
But if your app is not
basic, if it's not simple,

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.890
your success is at risk.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.150
And there are
certain constraints

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:32.020
that every person that works
with Shiny needs to be aware of.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:36.820
There are technical and
operational challenges

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.550
waiting for you ahead.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:44.800
And we've seen some
great apps fail.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:52.600
And if it's navigated
correctly, these challenges

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.510
can be resolved,
risk can be avoided,

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:59.740
and we think that
here we've identified

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.370
what makes Shiny successful.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:05.640
So

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.610
if you want to make
a successful app,

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.580
you need to make sure that
people are going to use it

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.015
and to be happy to use it.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:26.370
Right now, people really don't
want to use slow or not great

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.600
looking Shiny app, or
any app.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.840
People's expectations are very
high regarding the front end.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.830
And I'm not saying your
application is ugly.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:44.040
I just say some applications are,
and that the better it looks,

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:48.120
the easier it is to use, the
higher the chances of getting

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.780
buy in from the people
who are going to use it

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.870
at the end of the day.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.380
And we've seen some
teams building apps

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.530
for many months
struggling to get

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.760
to have an impact and apps
being bigger and bigger, slower

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:12.240
and slower, and apps getting
harder to maintain every day.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:15.330
Of course, we also
see teams which

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:19.680
manage to create great apps.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:26.060
It is possible, but it's
not always straightforward.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.300
And also, very often
it is not expertise

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:33.570
of this certain people
to work in such a way.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:45.930
So either you seem
to be either aware

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:48.720
that they might lose
speed of development

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.050
because it's hard to maintain
or they need to gain expertise

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.720
in other domains, which
are not their main domain.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:01.170
On the other hand,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.730
our background is in,

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:06.990
Appsilon was founded by
people with backgrounds

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:10.050
in applied mathematics
and software engineering,

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:15.030
and we are attracted
to R, we are attracted

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.350
to Shiny as software engineers.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:23.360
And we think that this
is something we bring to a

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:29.510
diverse community
of R world, and we

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:33.770
want to embrace our
backgrounds that were built

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:37.070
through the years, many
different Shiny apps,

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:39.020
we resolve many
different problems,

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:44.720
some of them taking months
to getting resolved.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.880
And we think that

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.420
we can share what we achieved.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:06:01.400
We believe that

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.150
our background in software
engineering and our tools

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.780
that we've created can help.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:13.830
Can be our way to contribute
to our community, and this way,

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:19.370
we can empower scientists,
researchers, data scientists

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:24.230
worldwide to create
spectacular apps themselves.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:27.740
We believe that this way
more apps can be created,

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.390
they can be created faster,

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:34.680
and also together, we can

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:40.950
encourage more people
to try R and Shiny and kick

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.590
start their journey.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:51.630
This is one slide that
I showed during my talk at

00:06:51.630 --> 00:07:00.190
UseR conference in Toulouse,
and at that time, I kind of

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:04.450
embraced advantages
of Shiny when

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:10.820
compared to different
technologies and alternatives.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:13.280
But

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:17.500
I think that solution to

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.780
building better Shiny
applications also

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.360
lies in these
other technologies,

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.200
and as much as we
have some advantages,

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:31.300
we also need to recognize that
certain other technologies can

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:35.550
teach us, how can we do better?

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:46.940
So with with our mission
statement being said,

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:53.630
which is to empower
data scientists to create

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:55.350
the spectacular Shiny apps,

00:07:55.350 --> 00:08:01.490
I will hand off now to Marek to show you
some work that we've actually

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:05.630
put into fulfilling
this mission and also

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.410
to discuss our progress

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.630
so far.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:13.970
Thank you Filip, so I'm very excited
to share with all of you

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:17.840
these new developments
that we will be releasing.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:21.140
And if you have any questions,
ask them in the questions

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:24.260
section, and we will
be happy to answer.

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:28.160
So a great UI is something
that takes your Shiny app

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:32.120
to an
entirely new level.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.549
And we have heard
great talks today

00:08:34.549 --> 00:08:39.080
from Tom and Shannon on
bslib and the theming

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.909
from Pedro about
custom styling

00:08:40.909 --> 00:08:44.570
from Dominik, who mentioned
lots of Shiny extensions.

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:49.280
And so so there are a lot
of great options already.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:52.070
However, looking at
what is available

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:55.310
and other technologies,
they have not only

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:57.200
bootstrapping
semantic, for example,

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:01.520
and actually, there are tons of
libraries for user interfaces

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.910
and so on and

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:08.180
not all of them are yet
easily available for us in Shiny.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:12.230
So so we are changing
a part of that today

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:18.050
with two new packages which are
shiny fluent and shiny react.

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:20.510
And the main thing
I want to talk

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:24.300
about today is shiny fluent.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:29.930
So shiny fluent lets you use
Microsoft fluent UI and Shiny.

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.480
But what is fluent
UI from Microsoft?

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:36.740
First of all, so fluent is a
UI library from Microsoft that

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:40.580
is used a lot in their software.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.100
If you use fluent UI
in your app, it

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:49.290
can basically look like
Microsoft Excel or Outlook.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:50.420
And so on.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:54.260
So this means that your app gets
a nice professional look, which

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:57.800
is a great fit, particularly
if your users are

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.680
in large companies,
and especially so

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:04.100
if your company is already
using Office and, for example,

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.780
PowerBI a lot.

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:10.410
So we've seen Shiny compete
with Power BI quite a lot.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:14.240
And almost always one of
the points in favor of PowerBI

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:16.670
was a better interface.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:17.340
So today

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.330
we want to bring this to Shiny.

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:26.470
So let me open another page.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:36.020
Right, so everything you see
here is a Shiny application,

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:38.990
it's completely
coming from Shiny.

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:43.760
And this is built using fluent UI.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:45.810
This is actually a part
of our documentation.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:48.770
So you can browse
all the components

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:53.370
that are available
in shiny fluent,

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:58.700
the various kinds of
buttons, for example.

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:00.270
It's very configurable.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:04.730
So you can really have a
lot of room in defining

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:08.600
easily using parameters
the appearance

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.760
and the behavior
of those buttons.

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:20.660
You have you have lots of cool
stuff like contextual menu,

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:24.710
nice, nice looking lists.

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:26.870
I really like some
of the features

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:30.920
that fluent provides
for guiding your users

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:36.190
through the application,
especially for the first time,

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:39.860
like coach mark, that
can appear and contain

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:41.725
some explanation and

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:48.260
of course, there are
nice, nice dialogue, windows.

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:51.130
And so on, just all
of it looks nice

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:57.850
and so powerful and
configurable. And so also shiny

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:04.300
fluent has a lot of components
for which often in Shiny, we

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:09.490
used to have to pull in other
packages like custom packages

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:15.600
to have some
kind of nice input.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:18.910
So a lot of this is
already available

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:25.780
within fluent and
via shiny fluent in Shiny.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:29.020
So there is one
problem, though, here,

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:35.740
and it is that fluent
is based on React

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:40.840
to get all of the appearance,
you need to render it by React.

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:47.250
And that's why we built shiny
react, which brings React to Shiny.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:50.460
And the story of shiny react
starts when you thought just

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:52.290
about how cool it
would be to have

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.450
a reliable and
efficient way of using

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:58.620
all the richness of the React
ecosystem in Shiny.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:12:59.610
Just think of it.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:00.940
There's so much.

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:03.030
And great stuff.

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:05.060
There's so much great
stuff available in React.

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:08.010
Like all the framework,
materialUI, blueprint, fluent,

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:13.170
and tons of components for
charts, maps and whatnot.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:17.820
And also one thing
that is super important

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:21.630
is that Facebook's team
has put a lot of effort

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:24.870
into improving its performance.

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:28.470
So it's really fast.

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:32.480
So it can make also
our Shiny apps faster.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:37.660
JavaScript developers have all of
this because they use React.

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:40.530
So we should have that, too.

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:42.640
So in one sentence,
shiny react aims so that

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:47.160
you easily use
React libraries in Shiny.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:52.260
And I'd like to thank the authors
of a package that was available

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:56.310
earlier, which is reactR,
especially for some

00:13:56.310 --> 00:14:00.780
of the ideas that they have in
their package, which helped us

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:03.480
and our scenario and our goal.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:09.990
It didn't work for us due
to some of the limitations.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:12.270
And one example
here is that reactr

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:16.650
seems to be focused
more on making

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:21.100
the porting
individual components.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:25.550
Or just a few components and not
necessarily entire UI libraries.

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:31.450
So what's special
about Shiny react?

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:36.310
It's, first of all, you
can port entire libraries

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:39.850
and it's easy for Shiny app
developers like the inputs

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:43.390
that you get that are
pretty close, as close as

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:49.770
possible to the Shiny API
that we are used to.

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:52.560
I think that was quite,
quite challenging to achieve

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:57.060
is to give the ability
to mix and nest Shiny

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:00.700
in React and inside
of Shiny again

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:02.400
as the outputs from Shiny.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:07.190
And so on, so that we can still
use all the Shiny components

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:09.010
that we are used to.

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:14.480
It also provides documentation
for the components coming

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:16.150
from the original packages.

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:20.880
But inside the R documentation
system, which is very useful,

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:23.850
and basically solves a lot
of practical challenges

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:29.220
that you just don't
have to think about.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:32.880
So this is what
made shiny fluent possible,

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:37.080
and I'm excited to see
what other libraries can

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.680
be made available this way.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:41.290
But let's go back to Shiny fluent.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:44.370
So this is a most basic app.

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:49.270
And what's special here is
just you just use the library.

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:54.120
So we load the package shiny
fluent and then

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:57.480
you just have to wrap
your code and work

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:00.180
with the reactant function call.

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:02.490
And inside, you can
use all the components

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:06.720
that are taken from
fluent, that you can

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:08.790
use all of their properties.

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:11.310
And so on.

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:15.380
So basically this code gives
you these two nice buttons.

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:20.780
But so that you
fully believe me,

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:22.740
I would like to do a quick live demo.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:30.030
So this is an application
that we built just

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:36.275
in a couple of hours using
fluent UI, it's again, all Shiny.

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:43.150
So a scenario that we
picked is that, let's say

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:47.650
we want to analyze how our
sales department performs

00:16:47.650 --> 00:16:53.950
and which sales reps, sales
representatives perform best.

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:59.950
So you can see here all
those nice inputs that I

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:03.400
think are very, very just

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:07.965
pleasant to use, it's
always an issue.

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:14.180
So I really like how
they solve this in fluent.

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:24.170
The really nice components
like this people picker,

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:27.519
which can show you a lot
of details about the

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:31.590
the people that you pick.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:37.650
Really nice list view.

00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:40.620
But
what's also interesting

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:42.900
is that what you
can see here, this

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:47.880
is a regular leaflet inserted
with a leaflet output,

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:52.270
but this leaflet output is
inside an entire structure,

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:58.590
which was put here not like
we used to do, but by react.

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:03.230
So it's not obvious that
this works, believe me.

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:05.220
So this is really
cool that you can

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:08.040
show that you can embed
what we already have

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:10.080
in Shiny inside react.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:15.630
And then the same here
with this block, that block,

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.120
and so,

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:19.750
so this is a truly live demo.

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:23.220
Let's try to make a
change to this app.

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:24.840
Add some functionality.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:27.060
Let's see if we succeed.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:32.610
So let's say we want
to filter this list

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:37.398
of deals closed
by the sales reps.

00:18:37.398 --> 00:18:38.940
We want to filter
out to the smallest

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:47.080
deals like the minimum limit
for the amount of the deal.

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:50.200
So we can use,

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:56.390
let's find some
component that we want to use.

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:58.750
So we can pick
slider, for example,

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:01.560
and we can see that
slider is just,

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:05.070
we just need to insert
a slider and give it

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:09.810
an id, as is usually in Shiny.

